Hey Tim,

So there's a few things I want to highlight:
For fractionated experiments, if a fragment ion is measured in multiple fractions, then MSstats will pick the fraction with the most measurements across all samples.  If there is a tie (e.g. in your case, if a fragment ion is measured in both fractions across all three biological replicates), then MSstats will pick the fraction with the greatest sum intensity (which seems to be your intention).
summaryForMultipleRows is only applied for cases when there are duplicate PSMs in a single MS run.  So this parameter doesn't modify the fraction selection behavior described in the first bullet point above.
Thanks,
Tony

